---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="Observability">
  <h1>Observability</h1>
  <p>
    Identity exposes structured logs on stdout, Prometheus metrics on
    <code>:9090/metrics</code>, HTTP health endpoints on
    <code>/health</code> + <code>/healthz</code>, and built-in
    OpenTelemetry tracing you can point at any OTLP collector.
  </p>

  <h2>Tracing</h2>
  <p>
    The server ships OpenTelemetry tracing. When enabled, an
    <code>otelconnect</code> interceptor wraps the Connect handlers so
    each RPC produces a server span, and outbound work (mailer, IDV, the
    OAuth token-endpoint POST) is instrumented with client-kind spans.
    Spans carry the <code>identity</code> service-name resource
    attribute and are exported over OTLP to a collector you supply — no
    sidecar required. When disabled, OTel's no-op tracer provider is
    installed and the hot path pays no per-request cost (the W3C
    TraceContext propagator is still installed so inbound trace IDs
    surface in logs).
  </p>
  <p>
    Tracing is configured with the <code>GATEWAY_OTEL_*</code> variables —
    enable/disable, OTLP exporter endpoint and protocol, sample ratio, and
    resource attributes. See the
    <a href="/identity/docs/installation/configuration#opentelemetry">OpenTelemetry
    section of the configuration reference</a> for the full list with current
    defaults.
  </p>
  <p>
    When <code>GATEWAY_OTEL_ENABLED</code> is <code>true</code> but
    <code>GATEWAY_OTEL_EXPORTER_ENDPOINT</code> is empty the server fails
    fast at boot rather than silently dropping traces.
  </p>

  <h2>Logs</h2>
  <p>
    The container uses uber-go/zap in production mode (JSON, single-line).
    Each line is structured; key fields:
  </p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>level</code></td><td><code>info</code> / <code>warn</code> / <code>error</code> / <code>fatal</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>ts</code></td><td>RFC3339 timestamp</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>msg</code></td><td>Snake-case event name (e.g. <code>identity_service_started</code>)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>caller</code></td><td><code>file:line</code></td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Log export</h3>
  <p>
    Set <code>GATEWAY_OTEL_LOGS_ENABLED=true</code> and
    <code>GATEWAY_OTEL_LOGS_ENDPOINT</code> to a collector's OTLP/HTTP base URL
    (for example <code>https://collector.example.test:4318</code>) and the
    <code>identity</code> command also sends every log line it keeps to
    <code>&lt;endpoint&gt;/v1/logs</code>, beside the JSON on its own output.
    The two carry the same lines: the same level and the same sampling. It is
    off by default, and the SDK's own <code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT</code>
    does not turn it on. A <code>log_export_enabled</code> line at start names
    the collector's scheme and host.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Records carry the same identity as traces: <code>service.name</code>
      <code>identity</code>, <code>service.version</code> (the build's, or
      <code>GATEWAY_OTEL_SERVICE_VERSION</code>) and
      <code>deployment.environment.name</code> from
      <code>GATEWAY_OTEL_DEPLOYMENT_ENV</code>. The standard
      <code>OTEL_SERVICE_NAME</code> and <code>OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES</code>
      override or add to them on log records only.
    </li>
    <li>
      The endpoint must be an OTLP/HTTP port (typically 4318), not gRPC. The
      SDK's <code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS</code>, timeout, compression and
      certificate settings apply; its endpoint and protocol variables do not.
    </li>
    <li>
      Exported lines carry what identity logs: client IP addresses, user IDs
      and redacted email addresses. Size the collector's access control and
      retention for that, and prefer <code>https</code> to any collector that
      is not on the same host or private network.
    </li>
    <li>
      Logging never waits on the collector. Records queue in a bounded buffer
      and the oldest are dropped when it is full. A failing export is reported
      as <code>otel_export_failed</code> on the process's own output at most
      once a minute, and so is a failing trace export while the log export is on.
    </li>
    <li>
      Queued records are flushed on every exit, including <code>identity
      migrate</code> and a failed start, for at most 5 seconds.
    </li>
    <li>
      With the export enabled, an endpoint that is empty or not an absolute
      <code>http</code> or <code>https</code> URL (with a host and no query or
      fragment) stops the process at start with an error naming the variable.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The export is part of the <code>identity</code> command. A server that
    embeds <code>identityserver</code> keeps the logger it passes in.
  </p>

  <h3>Notable startup events</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Event</th><th>When</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>identity_service_starting</code></td><td>Process boot, before the datastore connection</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>jwt_key_ring_loaded</code></td><td>Production keys loaded successfully</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>jwt_signer_dev_key_generated</code></td><td>No JWT key file configured (<code>GATEWAY_JWT_KEYS_FILE</code>) — using a throwaway dev key, kid <code>dev</code> (warning)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>using_dev_totp_encryption_key</code></td><td>No <code>GATEWAY_TOTP_ENCRYPTION_KEY</code> set (warning)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>repo_driver_selected</code></td><td>The datastore driver chosen by <code>GATEWAY_REPO_DRIVER</code> (<code>postgres</code> / <code>sqlite</code> / <code>memory</code>) is built and connected. The <code>driver</code> field names the active backend.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>identity_service_started</code></td><td>HTTP listener accepting connections</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>identity_service_shutting_down</code></td><td>Received SIGTERM/SIGINT</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Refusal events (<code>auth_refused</code> and <code>rpc_refused</code>)</h3>
  <p>
    When bearer authentication, session validation, project scope checks,
    agent-machine or delegated OAuth checks, refresh-token rotation, tenant
    selection, or account merging refuses a request, Identity emits a
    structured warning log line (<code>msg: "auth_refused"</code> at the
    middleware layer or <code>msg: "rpc_refused"</code> at the service layer)
    and attaches the same <code>reason</code> code to the request's final
    <code>msg: "request"</code> access log line. Raw access tokens, refresh
    tokens, cookies, authorization codes, challenge signatures, and passwords
    are never logged; when a bearer JWT fails verification,
    <code>pkg/jwt.PeekClaims</code> base64url-decodes only the payload segment
    to extract non-secret claim identifiers for operator diagnosis.
  </p>

  <h4>Structured refusal fields</h4>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>msg</code></td><td><code>auth_refused</code> (middleware) or <code>rpc_refused</code> (service)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>reason</code></td><td>Stable machine-readable refusal reason code (tables below)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>host</code></td><td>Normalized request <code>Host</code> header (for example <code>auth.example.com</code>)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>request_project_id</code></td><td>Project resolved for the inbound request (from <code>Host</code>, <code>X-Project-Key</code>, or the default project)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>project_id</code></td><td>Project claim (<code>pid</code>) on the token or session</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>tenant_id</code></td><td>Tenant claim (<code>tid</code>) on the token or requested tenant selection</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>user_id</code></td><td>Subject (<code>sub</code>) or caller account ID when known</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>session_id</code></td><td>Session ID (<code>sid</code>) when present on the token or refresh record</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>issuer</code></td><td>Token issuer (<code>iss</code>) when present on a rejected bearer token</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>auth_time_age_s</code></td><td>Age in seconds since the session's recorded sign-in (<code>auth_time</code>)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>max_age_s</code></td><td>Configured step-up re-authentication window in seconds (on <code>sign_in_not_recent</code>)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>expiry_age_s</code></td><td>Seconds elapsed since token or refresh record expiration</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>path</code></td><td>HTTP/Connect request path (middleware refusals and request access log)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>remote_addr</code></td><td>Client address (middleware refusals and request access log)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>trace_id</code></td><td>W3C trace ID from the request context when present</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h4>Bearer middleware reason codes (<code>auth_refused</code>)</h4>
  <table>
    <tr><th><code>reason</code></th><th>When emitted</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>missing_bearer</code></td><td>Protected endpoint called without a <code>Bearer</code> token in <code>Authorization</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>token_malformed</code></td><td>Bearer token is not a three-segment JWT or its header/payload cannot be decoded</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>token_signature</code></td><td>JWT signing algorithm, key ID (<code>kid</code>), or cryptographic signature is invalid</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>token_expired</code></td><td>JWT <code>exp</code> is in the past or <code>nbf</code> is in the future</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>token_issuer</code></td><td>JWT <code>iss</code> does not match the configured issuer</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>token_audience</code></td><td>JWT <code>aud</code> does not contain the configured audience</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>token_purpose_rejected</code></td><td>Non-access token (for example a pre-auth MFA token with <code>purpose != ""</code>) presented as a bearer access token</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>token_tenant_rejected</code></td><td>JWT <code>tid</code> is empty or is not an active selectable tenant in the token's project</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>token_tenant_lookup</code></td><td>Selectable tenant status lookup failed (returns <code>503</code>)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>session_lookup_failed</code></td><td>Revocation store read failed in <code>mode=session</code> (returns <code>503</code>)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>session_revoked</code></td><td>JWT <code>sid</code> is missing, unknown, revoked, or belongs to another user in <code>mode=session</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>token_project_mismatch</code></td><td>JWT <code>pid</code> does not match the request's resolved project (<code>request_project_id</code>)</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h4>Service refusal reason codes (<code>rpc_refused</code>)</h4>
  <table>
    <tr><th><code>reason</code></th><th>When emitted</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>unauthenticated</code></td><td>Caller identity or session ID is missing, or the caller account no longer exists</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>session_lookup_failed</code></td><td>Session lookup failed while verifying human session authority</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>session_revoked</code></td><td>Caller's session is missing or revoked when managing delegated OAuth or agent machines</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>session_project_mismatch</code></td><td>Caller's session was minted in a different project than <code>request_project_id</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>sign_in_not_recent</code></td><td>Caller's sign-in (<code>auth_time</code>) is older than the required step-up window (<code>max_age_s</code>)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>machine_not_found</code></td><td>Agent machine ID is malformed or does not exist in the project</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>machine_revoked</code></td><td>Agent machine or delegated grant is revoked, unconfigured for the resource, or its owner is no longer admitted</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>challenge_expired</code></td><td>Agent machine challenge ID is malformed, missing, expired, or already consumed</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>signature_invalid</code></td><td>Agent machine proof signature or challenge binding fails Ed25519 verification</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>key_invalid</code></td><td>Agent machine or enrolled agent Ed25519 public key is malformed or duplicates the machine key</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>refresh_token_invalid</code></td><td>Refresh token is empty or does not match any stored record</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>refresh_token_expired</code></td><td>Refresh token has passed its <code>ExpiresAt</code> timestamp</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>refresh_session_revoked</code></td><td>Refresh token was already consumed (replay), breached session idle/absolute timeout, or belongs to a locked/inactive account</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>not_a_member</code></td><td>Caller is not an active member of the tenant named on <code>RefreshToken</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>tenant_suspended</code></td><td>Tenant named on <code>RefreshToken</code> is suspended</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>tenant_selection_disabled</code></td><td><code>RefreshToken</code> named a tenant while tenant selection is disabled on the server</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>merge_same_user</code></td><td><code>MergeAccounts</code> attempted to merge an account into itself</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>merge_target_invalid</code></td><td><code>MergeAccounts</code> survivor or target account failed credential or merge-eligibility checks</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2>Health endpoints</h2>
  <Code code={`# Liveness — always returns 200 if the process is up.
curl -sf http://localhost/health
curl -sf http://localhost/healthz`} lang="bash" />
  <p>
    Both are exempt from auth and CORS. There is no readiness probe
    distinct from liveness; if the datastore connection fails at boot, the
    process exits with <code>identity_server_init_failed</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Metrics</h2>
  <p>
    A separate listener on <code>GATEWAY_METRICS_PORT</code> (default
    <code>9090</code>) serves the standard
    <code>github.com/prometheus/client_golang</code> handler. The Go
    runtime metrics (heap, goroutines, GC) are exported by default; HTTP
    request metrics are added via the middleware chain.
  </p>
  <p>
    Credential emails (password reset, sign-in code, magic link, and the
    verification email a sign-in refused for an unverified address sends) are
    sent in the background, at most 128 at once per instance. One requested while
    all 128 are in flight is dropped, though the request still gets its usual
    answer. <code>identity_email_send_dropped_total&#123;op&#125;</code> counts every
    drop; the <code>email_send_dropped_at_capacity</code> warning is logged
    at most once per <code>op</code> per minute, with the number
    <code>dropped</code> since its previous line.
  </p>
  <Code code={`curl -s http://localhost:9090/metrics | head`} lang="bash" />

  <h2>Recommended dashboards</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Auth attempt rate</strong> grouped by RPC name — login, signup, refresh — split by success / failure.</li>
    <li><strong>Lockout rate</strong> from the <code>account_locked</code> audit-event log line.</li>
    <li><strong>JWKS fetches</strong> — high traffic on <code>/.well-known/jwks.json</code> typically means downstream services are not caching.</li>
    <li><strong>Datastore latency</strong> — request tail latencies should track datastore (Postgres/SQLite) tail latencies; a divergence usually means goroutine contention in the service layer.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Alerts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Auto-generated key in prod.</strong> Alert on any occurrence of <code>jwt_signer_dev_key_generated</code> or <code>using_dev_totp_encryption_key</code> in production logs.</li>
    <li><strong>Wrong backend in prod.</strong> Alert if <code>repo_driver_selected</code> reports anything other than <code>postgres</code> in production — <code>sqlite</code> is single-node only and <code>memory</code> is non-durable.</li>
    <li><strong>Spike in lockouts.</strong> A burst of <code>login_failure</code> followed by <code>account_locked</code> on multiple users typically means a credential-stuffing attack.</li>
    <li><strong>Credential mail shed.</strong> Alert when <code>identity_email_send_dropped_total</code> increases: users asked for a reset, code or link that was never sent, usually because a flood of requests from many addresses is filling the send slots.</li>
    <li><strong>Datastore unreachable.</strong> Repeated <code>identity_server_init_failed</code> at boot is a hard outage; the process exits and the orchestrator restarts.</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
